# How to write a good essay introduction

The introduction of the essay. The function of the Introduction is to serve as a'map' of the essay, outlining to your reader the main argument and points which you develop in your essay. Most introductions begin with an orientation in the form of a brief general statement that leads the reader into the topic showing how the specific topic relates to bigger issues or to the discipline field. This is followed by your thesis statement, which is your main argument. Then you present the main points that support this argument, again in a general form. A good introduction is engaging; it gets the reader thinking on the topic which you are discussing whilst at the same time clearly establishing your own position. In general, the introduction should NOT:

• contain long quotations or paraphrases from other sources;
• contain your personal experience (unless you are writing a personal essay);
• include general statements or assertions that are not backed up by evidence or examples;
• contain controversial or sensitive material; and
• contain information which is not directly relevant to your topic.

